\title[Analytic structure of the moduli space of flat tori]
{The Analytic Structure of the Moduli Space of Flat Tori}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop from first principles the metric geometry of the Teichm\"uller space and the moduli
space of marked flat tori. Beginning with the algebra of quasiconformal dilatations of
real-linear maps of the plane, we show that submultiplicativity of the dilatation is an exact
consequence of the multiplicativity of the Jacobian determinant, and use it to construct the
Teichm\"uller metric intrinsically. We then prove the identification
$\dT=\tfrac12\dH$: the Teichm\"uller metric of the once-marked torus is exactly half the
Poincar\'e metric of curvature $-1$, equivalently the extremal dilatation between two marked
tori is the exponential of their hyperbolic distance. Within this explicit model we establish
proper discontinuity of the mapping class group in its sharpest form, the orbifold structure of
the quotient with two inequivalent cone points and one cusp, an exact displacement identity
yielding the Teichm\"uller translation length $\log\lambda(g)$ of an Anosov class and the
complete length spectrum $\{\arcosh(n/2):n\in\ZZ,\ n\ge3\}$ with least element $2\log\varphi$,
the systolic functional with Hermite's constant $\gamma_2=2/\sqrt3$ attained exactly at the
hexagonal torus, the two-sided comparison
$|\dM(\rho,\tau)-\tfrac12\log(1/\sys\tau)|\le\tfrac12\log5$ together with Mahler compactness and
properness of the moduli space, and Minkowski's second theorem in the sharp invariant form
$1\le\sys\cdot\sys_2\le4/3$ with both extremal loci determined exactly.
\end{abstract}

